{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"3eae5938-137a-4104-97be-2d13205e79c5","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Arrange four identical right triangles inside a square. Each triangle has legs a and b and hypotenuse c.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"C1","content":"Pythagorean theorem: an area proof"},{"op":"diagram","id":"squares","region":"left","primitives":[{"kind":"line","points":[[0.1,0.1],[0.9,0.1],[0.9,0.9],[0.1,0.9],[0.1,0.1]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"outer square with side length a plus b"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.4,0.1],[0.9,0.4],[0.6,0.9],[0.1,0.6],[0.4,0.1]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"central square bounded by the four hypotenuses"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.25,0.06],"content":"a"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.65,0.06],"content":"b"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.06,0.35],"content":"b"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.06,0.75],"content":"a"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.64,0.3],"content":"c"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.5,0.5],"content":"central square"},{"kind":"line","points":[[0.1,0.15],[0.15,0.15],[0.15,0.1]],"stroke":"solid","meaning":"right angle between the legs of one triangle"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"3eae5938-137a-4104-97be-2d13205e79c5","step":{"id":"s2","script":"The central angles are right angles because each triangle's two acute angles sum to ninety degrees. So the central area is c squared.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"angles","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"180^\\circ-(\\alpha+\\beta)=90^\\circ"},{"op":"equation","id":"area","anchor":{"el":"angles","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"(a+b)^2=4\\left(\\frac{ab}{2}\\right)+c^2"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"3eae5938-137a-4104-97be-2d13205e79c5","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Expand the outer square's area. The four triangles together contribute two a b, which appears on both sides.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"expanded","anchor":{"el":"area","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"a^2+2ab+b^2=2ab+c^2"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"What remains after subtracting 2ab from both sides?","expected_gist":"a squared plus b squared equals c squared."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"3eae5938-137a-4104-97be-2d13205e79c5","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Cancel the matching triangle areas. The squares on the two legs have the same total area as the square on the hypotenuse.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"result","anchor":{"el":"expanded","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\boxed{a^2+b^2=c^2}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
